{
  "context": "For each adapter, wrap cursor usage in try/finally blocks. Follow the pattern\nalready established in StarRocks (starrocks/workload.py) which uses:\n  cursor = connection.cursor()\n  try:\n      cursor.execute(query)\n      ...\n  finally:\n      cursor.close()\n\nFor mogrify calls, reuse the existing cursor variable instead of creating a new one.\nFor Databend client leaks, add try/finally around BlockingDatabendClient usage.\n",
  "description": "Adversarial code reviews of PRs #27-33 identified cursor and connection resource\nleaks in 5 of 6 new platform adapters: QuestDB, Databend, pg_duckdb, pg_mooncake,\nand StarRocks. Common patterns:\n\n1. **Cursor not closed on exception**: When cursor.execute() raises in execute_query(),\n   the except block returns without calling cursor.close(). Under sustained query\n   failures (common in benchmarking), this leaks server-side cursors.\n2. **Orphaned cursors from mogrify()**: pg_duckdb and pg_mooncake create new cursors\n   via conn.cursor().mogrify() for credential escaping, but never close them.\n3. **Connection leaks in test/check methods**: Databend's test_connection() and\n   check_server_database_exists() create BlockingDatabendClient instances without\n   closing them.\n\nThese leaks accumulate during long benchmark runs and can exhaust server-side\nconnection/cursor limits.",
  "legacy": {
